# Its own timer, its own S3 prefix, its own retention, and never restored together with
# the main database (API.md §6.1). That requirement is about the restore, not about the
# cadence: the claims database is the one unrecoverable asset in the design, so it gets the
# same 15-minute RPO as the database that can be rebuilt, not a worse one.
#
# OnCalendar=daily with Persistent=true is what left claims/ empty for the life of the
# bucket. A fresh root volume has no timer stamp, so systemd treated the daily elapse as
# missed and fired the catch-up the moment bootstrap.sh enabled the timer — before rc-api
# had created the file. The run found nothing, exited 0, and the stamp it then wrote
# suppressed every retry until the next midnight. OnBootSec/OnUnitActiveSec has no calendar
# elapse to miss, so there is no catch-up to mis-fire and a failure is retried in 15
# minutes rather than in a day.

[Unit]
Description=SkipperDesk device-id claims snapshot every 15 minutes

[Timer]
OnBootSec=10min
OnUnitActiveSec=15min
AccuracySec=1min
Unit=skipperdesk-backup@claims.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
